---
import Footer from '../components/landing/Footer.astro';
import Nav from '../components/landing/Nav.astro';
import Landing from '../layouts/Landing.astro';
import '../styles/page.css';
---

<Landing
  title="Privacy: Dispatch"
  description="What Dispatch, its website, the hosted demo and the Claude plugin collect, and where your data goes."
  path="/privacy"
>
  <Nav />
  <main class="page">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <h1>Privacy</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Dispatch runs on your machine. We don’t have accounts, analytics or telemetry, and we
          never see your code or your board. Last updated 11 October 2026.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The app and its daemon</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Your code, tasks, runs, messages and memory stay on your machine: in your repository,
            or in a local database with a git receipt log you own.
          </li>
          <li>
            The app sends us nothing. There is no account, no telemetry and no crash reporting.
          </li>
          <li>
            To check for updates, the app downloads a release manifest from GitHub
            (<code>github.com/wsoule/dispatch/releases</code>). GitHub sees that request.
          </li>
          <li>
            License keys are checked on your machine, with no network call.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Services you choose to connect</h2>
        <p>
          Dispatch talks to these only when you set them up, using your own accounts and keys.
          Their own privacy policies apply.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Your coding agent’s provider</strong>, such as Anthropic or OpenAI, through the
            agent you run (Claude Code, Codex and others). Agents see the code and tasks you give
            them.
          </li>
          <li><strong>GitHub or your git host</strong>, when you push, pull or open pull requests.</li>
          <li><strong>Linear</strong>, if you turn on Linear sync.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>TypeSafe</strong>, if you add a TypeSafe key: task and message text is sent to
            TypeSafe for judgments, such as telling a new topic from a follow-up.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Teams</h2>
        <p>
          Teammates sync a board through a git branch you host, or through the Dispatch relay if
          your team switches to it. The relay stores and forwards signed, sealed operations
          between your team’s machines and can’t read what is sealed. Nothing goes to the relay
          unless your team chooses it.
        </p>

        <h2>The Claude plugin</h2>
        <p>
          The Dispatch.foo plugin for Claude Code and Cowork downloads the{' '}
          <code>@dispatch-foo/mcp</code> package from npm once, then talks only to the Dispatch
          daemon on your own machine over local HTTP. When Claude messages someone through it,
          Dispatch delivers the message: to a teammate through your team’s sync, as described
          above, or to an outside A2A agent your project owner registered. Nothing else leaves
          your machine.
        </p>

        <h2>The website and the hosted demo</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            dispatch.foo sets no cookies and runs no analytics. Our host keeps standard server
            logs, such as IP address and browser, to run the site.
          </li>
          <li>
            The hosted demo gives you a sandbox for 30 minutes. It resets after 30 minutes without
            activity, and nothing you do there is kept.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Email</h2>
        <p>
          If you write to <a href="mailto:hello@dispatch.foo">hello@dispatch.foo</a>, we keep the
          conversation to reply to you, and use it for nothing else.
        </p>

        <h2>Questions</h2>
        <p>
          Email <a href="mailto:hello@dispatch.foo">hello@dispatch.foo</a>. If this policy
          changes, the date at the top will too.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Landing>
